  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 725 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLow level easterly flow looks to bring another round low stratus \nand patchy/areas of dense fog again tonight. Areas along the \nContinental Divide, the southwest valleys, and the plains \neast/northeast of the Rocky Mountain Front/Big Belts will see the \ngreatest coverage and overall visibility impacts. The general flow\naloft will shift southwesterly in advance of a closed low moving \nonto the Pacific NW coast. This will bring variable cloudiness and\na slight warm up on Monday before the low heads into the Northern\nRockies and sends a wave of mostly mountain light snow moving in \nsouthwest to northeast fashion Monday night into Tuesday. \nAccumulations in the 1 to 4 inch range will generally favor the \nhigher terrain along the Continental Divide and the southwest \nmountains.\n\nEnsembles then highlight another closed low moving southeast out \nof the gulf of AK before slowing down and remaining near or just \noff the Pacific NW coast for much of the week. This will bring a \nsouthwesterly flow aloft again and send a series of weak \nshortwaves and periods of spotty light rain and snow into the \nNorthern Rockies for much of the remaining week. This system is \nprogged to move towards the Great Basin/SW conus by early next \nweek. Similar to many systems so far this fall, splitting and \nshearing will make for a challenging forecast. A further north \ntrack while maintaining strength would favor more snow and colder \ntemperatures heading into early next week. Roughly half of the \nensembles favor this. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\n...Dense fog increasing tonight into Monday morning...\n\nLow stratus has been slow to thin out early this afternoon, with \nanother night of light wind and continued lingering surface \nmoisture in the forecast. The stratus looks to increase in \ncoverage and lower again tonight for another round of fog in the \nsouthwest valleys. A dense fog advisory was added through Monday \nat noon to address the impacts from variable visibility down to a \nquarter mile or less and frost deposition creating slippery spots \non area roads. \n\nDense fog looks to also be a concern for valleys and plains \nlocations adjacent to the Big Belt Mountains, with HREF \nprobabilities supporting half mile visibility approaching 50% in \nsome cases. Held off issuing fog products here with higher clouds \nlooking to be thicker and arriving sooner than the southwest. This\narea will continue to be monitored for fog products heading into \nthe evening hours. \n\n...Light mountain snow Monday night into Tuesday...\n\nA cold core aloft will bring a period of accumulating snow Monday\nnight into early Tuesday. H700 temperatures quickly drop off to \naround -5C, which will be supportive of the 1 to 4 inches snow \nbeing depicted by model guidance. Areas along the Continental \nDivide and the southwestern mountains along and south of I90 look \nto see the heaviest snowfall amounts with lighter snows over the \ncentral ranges. Impacts will mostly be slippery passes and reduced\nvisibility. - RCG",
